I have a set of spans, each with a start and end value. I want to determine the non-overlapping spans. A span is considered overlapping if its start value is less than or equal to the end value of another span.
Here is an example of the input and expected output:
```
Input:
[
  {start: 1, end: 3},
  {start: 2, end: 4},
  {start: 5, end: 7},
  {start: 6, end: 8},
  {start: 9, end: 10}
]

Output:
[
  {start: 1, end: 3},
  {start: 5, end: 7},
  {start: 9, end: 10}
]
```
Here is a JavaScript solution:
```
function nonOverlappingSpans(spans) {
  if (!spans.length) return [];

  spans.sort((a, b) => a.start - b.start);

  const result = [spans[0]];

  for (let i = 1; i < spans.length; i++) {
    if (spans[i].start > result[result.length - 1].end) {
      result.push(spans[i]);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```
This solution first sorts the spans by their start value. Then it iterates over the sorted spans and adds each span to the result array if its start value is greater than the end value of the last span in the result array. This ensures that the spans in the result array do not overlap.

You can test this function with the example input:
```
const input = [
  {start: 1, end: 3},
  {start: 2, end: 4},
  {start: 5, end: 7},
  {start: 6, end: 8},
  {start: 9, end: 10}
];

const output = nonOverlappingSpans(input);
console.log(output);
// Output:
// [
//   { start: 1, end: 3 },
//   { start: 5, end: 7 },
//   { start: 9, end: 10 }
// ]
```
This solution has a time complexity of O(n log n) due to the sorting, where n is the number of spans. The space complexity is O(n) for the result array.

